WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.140 --> 00:00:04.400
Please welcome father and son Odd Nerdrum and Øde Nerdrum!

00:00:24.220 --> 00:00:25.700
Welcome!

00:00:28.640 --> 00:00:33.240
First of all, because I have father and son here now ...

00:00:33.820 --> 00:00:36.460
I thought I would start with a painting ...

00:00:36.880 --> 00:00:38.880
... that you've painted, Odd.

00:00:38.920 --> 00:00:43.320
And it's a painting we can see here, which is called The Life Saver.

00:00:43.720 --> 00:00:46.460
And this is the beginning ...

00:00:46.660 --> 00:00:49.800
It's based on the beginning of your story.

00:00:50.120 --> 00:00:52.360
What is this painting about, Odd?

00:00:52.920 --> 00:00:56.120
Yes, well it was ...

00:00:56.780 --> 00:01:02.320
It happened that Øde became seriously ill.

00:01:02.320 --> 00:01:05.540
He was infected by his brother, from kindergarten.

00:01:06.200 --> 00:01:11.380
And after three-four weeks he was so sick that he had to ...

00:01:11.380 --> 00:01:15.020
... go to the doctor. The doctor thought he would live through it and then ...

00:01:15.020 --> 00:01:18.200
... he became paler and paler and in the end he was ...

00:01:18.600 --> 00:01:22.060
He almost looked like a waxed candle, in the face.

00:01:22.080 --> 00:01:27.940
I was away, and when I got home I could see that he was dying.

00:01:27.960 --> 00:01:33.160
I finally, thank God, got a hold of the private hospital where we were members.

00:01:33.720 --> 00:01:37.340
And in the end, after a lot of commotion ...

00:01:38.800 --> 00:01:44.700
... I got them to come over. And when he saw him then, he said that Øde was almost dead.

00:01:45.740 --> 00:01:48.620
And Øde died in ...

00:01:48.920 --> 00:01:52.820
... the car that drove with sirens up to the hospital.

00:01:53.320 --> 00:01:56.300
He died then. And he was dead for a while.

00:01:56.900 --> 00:01:59.940
And he got to the hospital, and they started saving him.

00:02:00.000 --> 00:02:01.500
He was in a car with a doctor?

00:02:01.600 --> 00:02:05.220
Yes, with a doctor from the private hospital.

00:02:05.760 --> 00:02:08.020
So ... So the case was that ...

00:02:08.200 --> 00:02:11.300
He was dead for quite a period of time.

00:02:11.300 --> 00:02:13.840
And when he later was baptised, I said that ...

00:02:14.320 --> 00:02:15.820
In the speech ...

00:02:15.960 --> 00:02:18.520
That he had been by the land of the dead.

00:02:19.020 --> 00:02:23.020
And I was very interested in what he would tell us about that later in life.

00:02:23.500 --> 00:02:27.160
I remember one time I was going on a trip with a show called Gutta på Tur.

00:02:27.160 --> 00:02:33.260
And then he absolutely wanted to come fetch me at this place where there was a sailboat that was coming to get us.

00:02:33.780 --> 00:02:36.420
And that's when I really understood ...

00:02:36.420 --> 00:02:39.160
... that our friendship had begun.

00:02:39.340 --> 00:02:41.080
Before that, he was a baby ...

00:02:41.720 --> 00:02:45.680
... who made strange faces at night.

00:02:46.160 --> 00:02:50.240
And I was very worried that he was damaged from that incident.

00:02:50.580 --> 00:02:52.400
And ...

00:02:52.820 --> 00:02:57.260
Because it is horrible if something happens like that.

00:02:57.600 --> 00:03:02.380
And that is what I was always worried about, something happening, and it started ...

00:03:02.580 --> 00:03:05.940
But we, thank God, found out that it was Tourettes.

00:03:06.320 --> 00:03:09.160
Which you also have?

00:03:09.160 --> 00:03:12.760
Yes, I have had a quite severe case of Tourettes, but I've managed to keep it contained ...

00:03:12.960 --> 00:03:14.860
... for the most part.

00:03:14.980 --> 00:03:19.220
But it doesn't go away, it turns into OCD instead.

00:03:19.220 --> 00:03:23.080
And that means that I get a lot of anxiety for people, and for crowds.

00:03:23.080 --> 00:03:26.140
I'm not anxious here, because here, I'm in control.

00:03:26.300 --> 00:03:30.780
But in a crowd, it doesn't turn out well.

00:03:30.780 --> 00:03:32.840
Øde, everything went well with you?

00:03:33.040 --> 00:03:33.760
Yes.

00:03:34.220 --> 00:03:38.300
And now you're sitting here. And you've done a documentary ...

00:03:38.800 --> 00:03:40.320
... about your father.

00:03:40.380 --> 00:03:40.880
Yes.

00:03:40.880 --> 00:03:42.200
And you've done this together with your brother?

00:03:42.260 --> 00:03:42.760
Yes.

00:03:42.860 --> 00:03:47.660
And there are six episodes, and he's not in it.

00:03:48.220 --> 00:03:50.100
No, he's not in it, that's the whole point.

00:03:50.320 --> 00:03:52.160
Why is that the whole point?

00:03:52.260 --> 00:03:53.060
Well ...

00:03:53.060 --> 00:03:56.720
The strange thing, especially with Norwegians, is that they ...

00:03:56.720 --> 00:04:00.480
... they don't appreciate what a person has done before he has died.

00:04:00.480 --> 00:04:03.040
While he's still alive, they just bother him.

00:04:03.040 --> 00:04:07.020
But when he dies, they will suddenly notice that he did something, right.

00:04:07.040 --> 00:04:09.280
And that's the focus we want.

00:04:09.480 --> 00:04:14.660
So you're making a documentary as if he no longer was alive?

00:04:14.660 --> 00:04:16.560
Yes, you could say that. Or ...

00:04:16.640 --> 00:04:20.120
It's a respect to his work.

00:04:20.120 --> 00:04:22.060
So in that way ...

00:04:22.220 --> 00:04:24.440
It's about his art and not about him.

00:04:24.600 --> 00:04:25.740
Yes. You could say that.

00:04:25.740 --> 00:04:30.940
Well, it is about him, but not in a way where we shove the microphone up his face and command him to answer.

00:04:32.140 --> 00:04:33.260
It is ...

00:04:33.260 --> 00:04:35.980
It is a hunt. So the title is The Hunt for Nerdrum.

00:04:35.980 --> 00:04:38.600
What's the most important ...

00:04:38.760 --> 00:04:41.080
... thing you've learned from Odd?

00:04:45.540 --> 00:04:48.640
The most important thing I've learnt ... It might be that ...

00:04:49.220 --> 00:04:52.260
... that I can choose what time I want to live in.

00:04:52.500 --> 00:04:55.380
That I don't have to settle for the present.

00:04:55.600 --> 00:04:57.080
What do you mean with that?

00:04:57.080 --> 00:05:01.620
You're thrown into a time, like we're thrown into the 21st century, right?

00:05:01.620 --> 00:05:04.340
If we were thrown into the 16th century, what would we do then?

00:05:04.340 --> 00:05:06.500
Would we perhaps be completely different?

00:05:06.500 --> 00:05:10.200
And the ... The point is that you don't get to choose when you live.

00:05:10.420 --> 00:05:17.220
And I've learnt from my father that I can study history, and I can choose a time period that I'm happy living in.

00:05:17.220 --> 00:05:19.080
It's like acting, it's what we do!

00:05:19.080 --> 00:05:20.440
Yes. And then I can live like that, right.

00:05:20.840 --> 00:05:24.400
It's not only how you dress ...

00:05:24.880 --> 00:05:28.880
It's also how you act, and how you deal with moral questions.

00:05:28.880 --> 00:05:33.560
So people who study history are less prejudiced because they have a wider perspective.

00:05:33.560 --> 00:05:34.980
And it's this perspective ...

00:05:35.220 --> 00:05:36.960
What time period do you live in, then?

00:05:38.640 --> 00:05:41.620
I haven't quite reached a conclusion. But I know I'm not living in the present time period.

00:05:41.820 --> 00:05:46.320
So do you agree on what time period you are in?

00:05:46.840 --> 00:05:49.000
Yes, most of the time.

00:05:49.200 --> 00:05:51.920
I'm quite smitten with the Age of Rococo.

00:05:51.920 --> 00:05:56.400
But my father perceives that as a perfumed parody of a time period, so ...

00:05:59.820 --> 00:06:04.380
So there are some disagreements. But we agree that the present is ...

00:06:04.740 --> 00:06:07.500
... that we shouldn't settle for the present, at least.

00:06:07.520 --> 00:06:09.280
But does that mean that ...

00:06:09.780 --> 00:06:13.140
Do you live completely cut off from everything ...

00:06:13.140 --> 00:06:15.080
... modern, as much as you can?

00:06:15.320 --> 00:06:17.460
Or are you on Facebook?

00:06:17.640 --> 00:06:18.420
No.

00:06:19.280 --> 00:06:22.920
I haven't been part of that adventure.

00:06:23.320 --> 00:06:26.880
Because I know that that is a tiny fish pond.

00:06:26.880 --> 00:06:29.640
And in there there are a few small goldfish.

00:06:29.640 --> 00:06:32.620
And those are the ones that are Facebook users.

00:06:32.740 --> 00:06:34.500
It's a small little world.

00:06:34.500 --> 00:06:40.100
And while they themselves think that they are speaking to the world, they are in reality just speaking to a small goldfish pond.

00:06:40.420 --> 00:06:46.400
Has he, while you were growing up, done any of the usual father-like things ...

00:06:46.720 --> 00:06:48.840
... do you go on holidays, for instance?

00:06:48.840 --> 00:06:50.400
No, we don't go on holidays.

00:06:50.400 --> 00:06:53.180
Holidays are for the lower classes, I learned that early.

00:06:54.720 --> 00:06:56.480
It's at least very tiring!

00:06:56.480 --> 00:06:57.260
Yes.

00:06:58.080 --> 00:07:01.120
They only do it to feel like they can afford it.

00:07:01.460 --> 00:07:02.260
No, that ...

00:07:02.600 --> 00:07:08.140
Has he been a, and Odd, you're now going to sit here and just be the object ...

00:07:08.340 --> 00:07:12.900
Has he been a father who has attended parent meetings, for instance?

00:07:12.900 --> 00:07:14.180
Uh, no.

00:07:14.180 --> 00:07:17.400
No, he has done everything else, but not parent meetings.

00:07:17.400 --> 00:07:18.080
But all the other things?

00:07:18.240 --> 00:07:18.740
Yes.

00:07:19.800 --> 00:07:22.320
So in that sense, he has also been a regular father?

00:07:22.740 --> 00:07:25.540
Yes, or I can't ... I guess so!

00:07:25.580 --> 00:07:29.180
That's not something I can answer, because I wasn't ..

00:07:30.940 --> 00:07:34.220
I don't really relate to my childhood in that way.

00:07:35.500 --> 00:07:39.580
I got to know him more in my teenage years.

00:07:40.000 --> 00:07:44.400
Why don't you attend parent meetings, Odd. You've got seven kids!

00:07:45.700 --> 00:07:48.740
I think parent meetings are scary because ...

00:07:49.240 --> 00:07:51.560
... it's so sentimental there.

00:07:53.060 --> 00:07:53.980
How?

00:07:54.480 --> 00:07:59.840
It's this ... I think that emotions should be directed outwards and not inwards.

00:07:59.840 --> 00:08:01.900
Because inwards, it's so dark.

00:08:02.940 --> 00:08:09.660
And with these child things, I have the impression that there's a lot of talk about inwards stuff.

00:08:09.860 --> 00:08:14.740
That's difficult to be a part of, so I have avoided that.

00:08:14.740 --> 00:08:16.860
My wife has done it excellently.

00:08:17.140 --> 00:08:19.840
Do you like parent meetings?

00:08:22.120 --> 00:08:23.120
No.

00:08:23.580 --> 00:08:30.000
I can be really shy! I haven't been to many parent meetings over the years, but ...

00:08:30.140 --> 00:08:33.180
But we usually have separate conversations.

00:08:33.180 --> 00:08:36.560
I think it's quite difficult to be in a large group, I can get shy.

00:08:36.560 --> 00:08:39.360
I tend to put on my head phones and pretend that I'm listening to music.

00:08:39.440 --> 00:08:41.280
Before the meetings start.

00:08:41.300 --> 00:08:47.040
So I still can hear what's happening, but then I won't get disrupted.

00:08:47.040 --> 00:08:49.440
And you don't have to get to know all the other parents?

00:08:49.440 --> 00:08:54.360
Yes. I think that small conversations are really difficult.

00:08:54.360 --> 00:08:57.900
I often become too honest and too direct.

00:08:57.960 --> 00:09:00.440
And then the atmosphere can become weird.

00:09:00.860 --> 00:09:03.620
So you can relate to what Odd says about ...

00:09:03.620 --> 00:09:06.040
... this social anxiety?

00:09:06.040 --> 00:09:09.580
A lot of times, it feels like ...

00:09:09.580 --> 00:09:11.340
It's an awkward situation for everyone.

00:09:11.360 --> 00:09:13.480
Yes, it becomes ...

00:09:13.900 --> 00:09:16.460
I like conversations in small groups.

00:09:16.460 --> 00:09:19.440
I think it's difficult when it becomes too big.

00:09:19.920 --> 00:09:24.200
There are also some men who join breathing exercises.

00:09:24.200 --> 00:09:26.260
Together with the pregnant one.

00:09:26.420 --> 00:09:31.000
Yes, before they give birth, when they're trying to learn how to breathe?

00:09:31.000 --> 00:09:33.700
And I haven't been part of any breathing exercises.

00:09:34.480 --> 00:09:35.380
No.

00:09:38.300 --> 00:09:39.660
We all do what we can.

00:09:39.740 --> 00:09:41.900
My father is extremely focused.

00:09:42.720 --> 00:09:44.160
And ...

00:09:44.480 --> 00:09:47.060
That's part of the point of doing this TV show, to get people to understand that.

00:09:47.260 --> 00:09:49.420
That we're not talking about ...

00:09:49.900 --> 00:09:53.540
Rembrandt may have painted 10 master pieces, but Odd may have painted 100.

00:09:53.740 --> 00:09:54.240
And ...

00:09:54.240 --> 00:09:55.740
People in the present time don't understand this.

00:09:55.860 --> 00:09:56.880
That was generous?

00:09:56.880 --> 00:09:59.580
No it's not generous, it's pretty factual.

00:10:01.340 --> 00:10:03.400
Within my field, this is quite factual.

00:10:03.400 --> 00:10:05.400
Those big words are not from me.

00:10:05.400 --> 00:10:07.860
I'm not allowed to say that because I'm the son.

00:10:08.940 --> 00:10:09.720
But ...

00:10:09.920 --> 00:10:13.760
Odd, you've said that you're a person who complains a lot.

00:10:15.100 --> 00:10:24.900
Yes, but I have to admit that I've lived a happy a life as a person is able to experience.

00:10:25.940 --> 00:10:28.180
And the reason for that is that ...

00:10:28.420 --> 00:10:32.760
I have a family, that Øde is part of ...

00:10:33.320 --> 00:10:36.180
... who protect me in a fantastic way.

00:10:36.280 --> 00:10:37.640
Especially my wife.

00:10:38.220 --> 00:10:38.800
So ...

00:10:39.100 --> 00:10:42.720
I've lived in a land of happiness, in a way.

00:10:42.720 --> 00:10:44.520
I think you deserve an applause for that!

00:10:44.640 --> 00:10:45.140
No!

00:10:52.340 --> 00:10:54.200
Before we finish, I have to ask you ...

00:10:54.400 --> 00:10:58.260
You've driven here from Helsingborg ...

00:10:58.620 --> 00:11:01.660
... where you now live. You still live at home?

00:11:02.260 --> 00:11:05.660
Yes. I live with my father and his pupils.

00:11:05.780 --> 00:11:07.480
He found the house.

00:11:07.760 --> 00:11:12.380
And there we could remove the whole floor.

00:11:12.580 --> 00:11:15.520
And we almost got a cathedral up.

00:11:16.100 --> 00:11:17.480
Extremely high up!

00:11:17.480 --> 00:11:18.880
And it became really nice.

00:11:19.160 --> 00:11:20.760
And you can see Denmark?

00:11:21.200 --> 00:11:24.040
I can see all the way to Copenhagen.

00:11:24.040 --> 00:11:28.380
And you can see that the earth is round, they way I have painted it many times.

00:11:29.340 --> 00:11:32.140
So that is very dream-like.

00:11:32.140 --> 00:11:35.540
Do you feel you've come full circle, now that you're back in your place of birth?

00:11:35.860 --> 00:11:39.400
No, I think the graveyard is very beautiful ...

00:11:39.720 --> 00:11:43.560
And I said to someone who was there once ...

00:11:43.560 --> 00:11:46.440
... that it's so beautiful, the graveyard.

00:11:46.440 --> 00:11:49.220
Those who lay there must have a wonderful time.

00:11:50.480 --> 00:11:53.900
And he replied: «Ugh. Ugh.»

00:11:54.080 --> 00:11:57.200
So I don't think that's the reason I went there.

00:11:57.560 --> 00:11:58.920
That's nice to hear.

00:11:59.760 --> 00:12:00.720
The headroom.

00:12:00.760 --> 00:12:02.680
It was beautiful there!

00:12:04.720 --> 00:12:07.920
Odd, Øde, thank you so much for being here today!

